Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 15:30:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The tanner window asked which tanner from a button, and a
 button has no npc

FIXES THE BUILD. ~tan_window_cost read npc_type to choose between Al Kharid's prices
and Canifis's. npc_type needs the active_npc pointer: an [opnpc3,...] trigger has it,
an [if_button,...] trigger does not, and the server build refused all 32 buttons at
once with "Attempt to access uninitialized pointer [ 'active_npc' ]".

The question is asked once now, in ~tan_window_open, where the npc is still in scope,
and the answer is kept in %tan_window_canifis - scope=temp, protect=no, the pair
skill_guide.varp and game_options.varp already carry for exactly this reason and say so
in their own comments.

FOURTH BUILD LOST TO A POINTER, and the third where the round checked one pointer and
not the other: the XP-lock round wrapped its [if_button] bodies in p_finduid for
p_active_player, this round did the same, and neither looked at active_npc.

A GENERAL rs2check RULE WAS WRITTEN AND MEASURED AND IS NOT IN THIS PATCH. Following
every button-family trigger through its calls to a read of npc_type gives NINE hits on
a tree that compiles, all of them through ~chatnpc - which the handoff already records
as working from an [if_button] and which this round could not explain. A lint with nine
false positives is the rule-19 situation again, and a checker that cries wolf gets
switched off. The compiler is the pointer checker; that this sandbox cannot run one is
the real gap, and it is worth saying in the handover rather than papering over with a
lint.

So windows_battery gains six narrow checks about this round's own files instead -
tan_window.rs2 reads npc_type exactly once, it is inside the opener, nothing after the
first button trigger in any of the four generated files reads it, and the varp is
scope=temp protect=no. windows_mutate gains two entries and is 21 of 21.

